the man accused of murdering five people  at a newspaper in Annapolis Maryland  made his alleged intentions clear before  the deadly rampage  police say the Jared Ramos sent  threatening letters to a courthouse the  Maryland Court of Appeals and a law  office just a few hours before the  attack the details in the letters are  very disturbing signed by suspected  gunman Jared Ramos the haunting letters  reveal he was on his way to the Capitol  Gazette with the objective of killing  every person present a copy of the  letters was sent to the paper's former  executive editor Tom Marquardt he  previously told CBS news about Ramos his  years of threats against the paper the  police had visited him but came away  with a conclusion that there wasn't  enough there to really find criminal  charges against him Ramos has letters  also referenced his failed defamation  lawsuit against the Capitol Gazette in  2012 he sued the newspaper after it  published a story about his harassment  of a former female classmate the suit  was eventually thrown out and an appeals  court refused to hear the case in his  letter to the appeals court he writes if  this is how the Maryland judiciary  operates the law now means nothing you  were too cowardly to confront those lies  and this is your receipt I told you so  he also sent a letter to Judge Charles  Moylan one of the appellate judges in  charge of the case welcome mr. Moylan to  your unexpected legacy you should have  died my brother was a person of  phenomenal grace last night more than  500 family members and friends gathered  to remember editor Rob hyacinth one of  the five capital Gazette employees who  died in the attack my dad is my best  friend he is my hero he is my strength  he is the purest form of happiness I've  ever felt in my life the American  Society of news editors has called for a  moment of silence in all newsrooms on  Thursday exactly one week after the  shooting annapolis mayor Gavin Buckley  says he put in a request to the White  House to lower American flags to half  staff across the country he says the  president didn't  his request we've reached out to the  White House about the mayor's request  and have not heard back a lot of warning  signs there you haven't it's not clear  it's not clear at the if the the editors  of the paper got the letters before the  deadly attack was was was carried out  he's sent the letters but did do they  receive the letters in time to read them  that still isn't clear to me right
 

